i ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973156/state.json" "file_sha256" "9a63c36db3fe086c0aeb460a1385588ae791834d3f29d78f1e533cdbef8a7a18"} {"question" "q-327880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4+1 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 4+1 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le x\\le 5.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{7}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973156/state.json" "file_sha256" "9a63c36db3fe086c0aeb460a1385588ae791834d3f29d78f1e533cdbef8a7a18"} {"question" "q-327880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973156/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b831a1b72106ea95d4e79ccbaf8f78b64c9b28342b22f65be5511b4eb3a1a38d"} {"q